description:
statusline._fetch_usage returns at the 'fresh' branch (src/ccreport/statusline.py:749) before it ever considers the cost summary:

    if fresh:
        return _adjust_passed_resets(row, now)
    ...
    elif cost_summary is None:
        _spawn_usage_refresh(..., costs_only=True, ...)

The usage row is a singleton, global to every project. The cost summary is per project (meta key 'cost_summary:<project_key>', COST_SUMMARY_MAX_AGE 900 s). So a session in project A that keeps the shared row fresh stops every session in project B from ever spawning the costs-only refresh that would rewrite B's summary. Observed: with concurrent sessions in three projects, cost_summary_time:-Users-ove-git-colophon was 1778 s old and :-Users-ove-git-infrastructure 2998 s old, both far past the 900 s max age, while the usage row stayed fresh.

What the user sees: week_model_costs has no usage-table column — the summary is its only route to the render (statusline.py:1911) — so the scoped per-model segment silently drops its cost and renders 'Fa:0%' instead of 'Fa:0% $4'. W falls back to the row's own week_cost column, which is a different, older number: $350 on screen against $652 in the summary.

Fix: on the fresh-row path, spawn the costs-only refresh when the render's summary has expired (cost_summary is None) and HISTORIC_COST is on. Gate it on the costs lock alone, not is_fetch_blocked() — try_acquire_costs_lock deliberately skips the API error backoff (cache_db.py:1350), since a failing API says nothing about whether local JSONL can be rescanned, and a gate that consulted it would starve the summary for the whole backoff.

